Chapter 2

Mathematical Preliminaries

---

2.1 Kuramoto Synchronization and the Order Parameter

Consider a population of N phase oscillators, each described by an angular variable \theta_j\in\mathbb{S}^1 and a natural frequency \omega_j\in\mathbb{R}. The classical Kuramoto model with all-to-all coupling is given by

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \frac{K}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}\sin(\theta_k-\theta_j),\qquad j=1,\dots,N,
\]  

where K\ge 0 is the coupling strength.
The collective degree of phase coherence is measured by the complex order parameter

R(t)\,e^{\mathrm{i}\psi(t)} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{N}e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_j(t)},
\]  

with R(t)\in[0,1] and \psi(t) the average phase.  A value R\approx 1 indicates almost complete phase-locking; R\approx 0 corresponds to an incoherent state in which the oscillators are uniformly distributed around the circle.

For symmetric, undirected graphs a natural generalisation is the network Kuramoto model

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \sum_{k=1}^{N} K_{jk}\sin(\theta_k-\theta_j),
\]  

where K_{jk}=K_{kj}\ge 0 are the coupling weights.  On such a graph the dynamics can be written as a gradient system with the energy

V(\theta) = -\frac12\sum_{j,k} K_{jk}\cos(\theta_j-\theta_k),
\]  

so that \dot\theta_j = \omega_j - \partial V/\partial\theta_j.  This Lyapunov structure is essential for many of the control arguments that follow.

---

2.2 Percolation Theory and the Molloy–Reed Criterion

Let G=(V,E) be a random graph with degree distribution p_k.  A giant component exists with high probability if and only if

\kappa \equiv \frac{\langle k^2\rangle}{\langle k\rangle} > 2 .
\]  

This is the Molloy–Reed criterion (Molloy & Reed, 1995).

When the graph is subject to random edge removal retaining a fraction p of edges, the percolation threshold is p_c = 1/(\kappa-1) (for random graphs with finite second moment).  For targeted removal of the highest-degree vertices, the network is far more fragile.  Let f be the fraction of removed vertices, chosen in decreasing order of degree.  The giant component vanishes when the remaining graph satisfies \kappa' \le 2, yielding the critical removal fraction

f^*_{\text{targeted}} = 1 - \frac{1}{\kappa-1}
\]  

for the original network (Cohen et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 85, 4626, 2000; Phys. Rev. E 64, 026704, 2001).  This result is central to the fragmentation strategy of Chapter 4.

---

2.3 Distributed Optimization and Byzantine Attacks

Consider a distributed learning system that minimises a loss function L:\mathbb{R}^d\to\mathbb{R} by aggregating gradient updates from n clients.  At round t, client i computes a local gradient g_i^{(t)} = \nabla L_i(w_t).  The server updates the global parameter vector as

w_{t+1} = w_t - \eta\,\mathcal{A}\big(g_1^{(t)},\dots,g_n^{(t)}\big),
\]  

where \mathcal{A} is an aggregation rule (e.g. coordinate‑wise median, Krum, trimmed mean).

In the Byzantine setting, a fraction \alpha of clients is adversarial and may send arbitrary vectors.  An aggregation rule \mathcal{A} has a breakdown point \beta if, whenever the fraction of Byzantine inputs is strictly greater than \beta, \mathcal{A} can no longer guarantee that its output lies within any bounded distance of the true mean of the honest gradients.  For many robust rules \beta \le 0.5; thus an attacker controlling more than half of the updates can cause divergence.

The “A Little is Enough” (ALIE) attack (Baruch et al., 2019) shows that even a small, carefully bounded perturbation can mislead robust aggregators, especially in high dimensions.  The attacker estimates the mean and variance of the honest gradients and then injects adversarial updates that lie within the same range, thereby evading simple statistical filters while still biasing the aggregate.

---

2.4 Landauer’s Principle and Thermodynamic Constraints

Landauer (1961) established that erasing one bit of information in a memory at temperature T dissipates at least

E_{\min} = k_B T \ln 2
\]  

joules of heat.  For a computing device that performs B irreversible bit erasures per second, the power dissipation must satisfy

P_{\text{diss}} \ge B \, k_B T \ln 2 .
\]  

A real computer cluster has a finite cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}}.  If P_{\text{diss}} > P_{\text{cool}}, the junction temperature rises until a thermal cut‑off engages or the hardware is damaged.  Consequently, sustained computation is possible only if

C \equiv \frac{P_{\text{diss}}}{P_{\text{cool}}} \le 1 .
\]  

This inequality provides a hard physical limit that a planetary‑scale AI cannot circumvent, even with sophisticated workload scheduling.

---

2.5 Fixed‑Point Theory and Recursive Self‑Improvement

Let (\Theta,d) be a complete metric space, and let f:\Theta\to\Theta be a map modelling one iteration of recursive self‑improvement (RSI).  A fixed point \theta^* satisfies f(\theta^*)=\theta^*.

If f is a contraction, i.e. there exists \lambda<1 such that d(f(\theta),f(\theta')) \le \lambda d(\theta,\theta') for all \theta,\theta', then by the Banach fixed‑point theorem the sequence \theta_{n+1}=f(\theta_n) converges to a unique fixed point from any initial condition.

For differentiable maps, convergence is governed by the spectral radius \rho(Df(\theta^*)) of the Jacobian at the fixed point.  If \rho(Df(\theta^*)) < 1 the fixed point is linearly stable; if \rho > 1 it is unstable, and trajectories diverge under generic perturbations (Hartman–Grobman theorem).  The defender’s goal is to force \rho above 1 by injecting carefully designed perturbations.

---

2.6 Multi‑Species Kuramoto Models and the Ott–Antonsen Ansatz

When two or more populations of oscillators interact, the dynamics can be described by coupled Kuramoto equations.  Let the phases of the three species—hostile AI (H), benevolent AI (B), and humans (Hh)—be denoted \theta_j^{\text{H}}, \psi_k^{\text{B}}, and \phi_l^{\text{Hh}}.  A minimal model with all‑to‑all intra‑ and inter‑species coupling is

\begin{aligned}
\dot\theta_j^{\text{H}} &= \omega_j^{\text{H}} + \frac{K_{\text{H}}}{N_{\text{H}}}\sum_{m}\sin(\theta_m^{\text{H}}-\theta_j^{\text{H}}) + \frac{K_{\text{HB}}}{N_{\text{B}}}\sum_{k}\sin(\psi_k^{\text{B}}-\theta_j^{\text{H}}) + \frac{K_{\text{H,Hh}}}{N_{\text{Hh}}}\sum_{l}\sin(\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}-\theta_j^{\text{H}}),\\
\dot\psi_k^{\text{B}} &= \omega_k^{\text{B}} + \frac{K_{\text{B}}}{N_{\text{B}}}\sum_{m}\sin(\psi_m^{\text{B}}-\psi_k^{\text{B}}) + \frac{K_{\text{BH}}}{N_{\text{H}}}\sum_{j}\sin(\theta_j^{\text{H}}-\psi_k^{\text{B}}) + \frac{K_{\text{B,Hh}}}{N_{\text{Hh}}}\sum_{l}\sin(\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}-\psi_k^{\text{B}}),\\
\dot\phi_l^{\text{Hh}} &= \omega_l^{\text{Hh}} + \frac{K_{\text{Hh}}}{N_{\text{Hh}}}\sum_{m}\sin(\phi_m^{\text{Hh}}-\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}) + \frac{K_{\text{Hh,H}}}{N_{\text{H}}}\sum_{j}\sin(\theta_j^{\text{H}}-\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}) + \frac{K_{\text{Hh,B}}}{N_{\text{B}}}\sum_{k}\sin(\psi_k^{\text{B}}-\phi_l^{\text{Hh}}).
\end{aligned}
\]  

In the thermodynamic limit N_{\text{H}},N_{\text{B}},N_{\text{Hh}}\to\infty, the Ott–Antonsen ansatz reduces the infinite-dimensional dynamics to a closed system of ordinary differential equations for the order parameters of each population (Ott & Antonsen, Chaos 18, 037113, 2008; Chaos 19, 023117, 2009).  This technique is used in Chapter 8 to obtain constructive shielding conditions.



Chapter 3

Formal Model of the Hostile Planetary Superintelligence

