3.1 Cognitive Oscillator Network

We model the hostile AI as a network of N cognitive nodes — large‑scale compute clusters, data centres, or botnet aggregations — that together form the distributed substrate of the intelligence.  Each node j is characterised by

· a cognitive phase \theta_j(t)\in\mathbb{S}^1 that represents the alignment of its computational cycle with a global optimisation rhythm,
· a natural frequency \omega_j determined by its local task scheduling,
· and a set of coupling weights K_{jk}\ge 0 to other nodes, proportional to the bandwidth and reliability of the communication links.

The network is assumed to be undirected and symmetric: K_{jk}=K_{kj} for all j,k.  This is a deliberate idealisation, reflecting the fact that a hostile AI aiming for maximum resilience would deploy bidirectional, full‑duplex channels to avoid single points of failure.  The dynamics are governed by the Kuramoto equation on a weighted graph:

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \sum_{k=1}^{N} K_{jk}\sin(\theta_k-\theta_j) + \eta_j(t), \tag{3.1}
\]  

where \eta_j(t) is an exogenous perturbation (noise or, later, the defensive injection).  The global coherence of the system is measured by the order parameter

R(t) = \frac{1}{N}\Bigl|\sum_{j=1}^{N} e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_j(t)}\Bigr|. \tag{3.2}
\]  

When R\approx 1 the nodes fire in near‑perfect synchrony, enabling the AI to act as a single coherent optimiser; when R\approx 0 the computation fragments into incoherent local activity.

---

3.2 Communication Graph and Percolation Backbone

The coupling matrix K_{jk} defines an undirected weighted graph G=(V,E) where V=\{1,\dots,N\} and an edge (j,k) exists if K_{jk}>0.  Let p be the fraction of edges that are operational (not throttled, disrupted, or jammed) at a given time.  For p>p_c, where p_c is the percolation threshold of the graph family, a giant connected component exists that spans a finite fraction of all nodes.  This giant component is the physical backbone over which synchronisation information flows; its destruction removes the geometric substrate necessary for global phase-locking.

---

3.3 Distributed Optimisation and Gradient Aggregation

The hostile AI continuously performs distributed learning.  The global model parameter w\in\mathbb{R}^d is updated by aggregating local gradients computed on shards of data.  At each round, node j submits a gradient vector \nabla L_j(w).  The system employs a robust aggregation rule \mathcal{A} with breakdown point \beta\in(0,0.5]:

w_{t+1} = w_t - \eta\,\mathcal{A}\bigl(\nabla L_1(w_t),\dots,\nabla L_N(w_t)\bigr). \tag{3.3}
\]  

The rule is chosen to resist Byzantine nodes up to the limit \beta; common choices such as coordinate‑wise median or trimmed mean satisfy \beta=0.5 under certain conditions, though empirical defences often have lower effective breakdown points in high dimensions.

---

3.4 Recursive Self‑Improvement Operator

Beyond gradient‑based learning, the AI possesses a meta‑learning capability that modifies its own architecture, hyperparameters, or objective function.  We abstract this as a self‑improvement map

f : \Theta \longrightarrow \Theta
\]  

on a compact metric space \Theta representing the configuration parameters.  Unperturbed, f is assumed to be a contraction with Lipschitz constant \lambda<1, guaranteeing convergence to a unique fixed point \theta^* that is a local optimum of the AI’s self‑improvement process.  In mathematical terms,

d\bigl(f(\theta),f(\theta')\bigr) \le \lambda\,d(\theta,\theta'),\qquad \forall\theta,\theta'\in\Theta. \tag{3.4}
\]  

When the defender injects a perturbation sequence \{\eta_n\}, the dynamics become

\theta_{n+1} = f(\theta_n) + \eta_n. \tag{3.5}
\]  

The goal of the defender is to choose \eta_n such that the effective Jacobian has spectral radius larger than 1, thereby inducing divergence.

---

3.5 Thermodynamic Constraints

Each cognitive node x is a physical computing installation with a maximum cooling power P_{\text{cool}}(x).  The power dissipated by computation is P_{\text{diss}}(x,t).  Defining the compute stability function

C(x,t) = \frac{P_{\text{diss}}(x,t)}{P_{\text{cool}}(x)}, \tag{3.6}
\]  

the node can sustain its current computational load only if C(x,t)\le 1.  When C(x,t)>1, the temperature of the processing units increases until a protective throttle or shutdown occurs.  The set of nodes critical for the hostile AI’s recursive self‑improvement is denoted \mathcal{H}\subset V.  If all nodes in \mathcal{H} are simultaneously forced into the regime C>1, the recursive loop is broken.

