Chapter 4

Layer 1 – Network Fragmentation

---

4.1 Fragmentation via Targeted Percolation

The hostile AI’s global coherence depends on the existence of a giant connected component in its communication graph.  If this giant component can be destroyed, synchronising signals can no longer propagate across the network, and the order parameter R(t) is forced toward zero.

Theorem 4.1 (Fragmentation threshold under targeted hub removal).
Let the hostile AI’s communication graph be a realisation of a configuration model with degree distribution p_k satisfying \kappa \equiv \langle k^2\rangle/\langle k\rangle > 2.  Let the defender remove (or isolate the incident edges of) the fN nodes with the highest degrees, where

f = f^*_{\text{targeted}} = 1 - \frac{1}{\kappa-1}. \tag{4.1}
\]  

Then, with probability tending to 1 as N\to\infty, the residual graph contains no giant component; every connected component has size O(\log N).

Proof.  After removal, the effective degree distribution is truncated above some degree k_{\max}(f).  The new moment ratio \kappa' is a decreasing function of f.  For f = f^*_{\text{targeted}}, one finds \kappa' = 2 exactly.  By the Molloy–Reed criterion, a giant component exists iff \kappa' > 2; therefore at f^*_{\text{targeted}} the giant component vanishes.  The O(\log N) component size bound follows from standard subcritical random graph results (Bollobás, Random Graphs, 1985). ∎

Remark.  For scale‑free networks with \kappa initially large, f^*_{\text{targeted}} can be as small as a few percent of all nodes (Albert et al., Nature 406, 378, 2000).  Thus targeted fragmentation is an extremely efficient first strike.

To sustain the fragmentation over time, the defender must ensure that the rate at which edges are disabled exceeds the hostile AI’s reconnection rate.  A dynamic extension is conjectured in Section 4.3.

---

4.2 Phase Desynchronisation as an Alternative: Order Parameter Gradient

Even when the communication graph remains largely intact, phase synchronisation can be disrupted by injecting small, intelligently directed phase perturbations.  This section develops the mathematical foundation for such an attack.

Lemma 4.2 (Static gradient of the order parameter).
For the Kuramoto order parameter

R(\theta) = \frac{1}{N}\Bigl|\sum_{j=1}^{N} e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_j}\Bigr|,
\]  

the partial derivative with respect to a single phase \theta_k is

\frac{\partial R}{\partial\theta_k} = \frac{1}{N}\,\operatorname{Im}\!\bigl(e^{-\mathrm{i}\psi} e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_k}\bigr),
\]  

where \psi = \arg\!\bigl(\frac{1}{N}\sum_j e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_j}\bigr) is the mean phase.

Proof.  Write Z = \frac{1}{N}\sum_j e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_j} = R e^{\mathrm{i}\psi}.  Then R = |Z| = \sqrt{Z\bar Z}.  Differentiating,

\frac{\partial R}{\partial\theta_k} = \frac{1}{2R}\Bigl( \frac{\partial Z}{\partial\theta_k}\bar Z + Z\frac{\partial\bar Z}{\partial\theta_k} \Bigr)
= \frac{1}{2R}\Bigl( \mathrm{i}e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_k} \frac{1}{N} \bar Z + Z \frac{1}{N}(-\mathrm{i}e^{-\mathrm{i}\theta_k}) \Bigr).
\]  

Using \bar Z = R e^{-\mathrm{i}\psi} and Z = R e^{\mathrm{i}\psi}, the expression simplifies to

\frac{1}{2R}\cdot \frac{R}{N}\bigl( \mathrm{i}e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_k} e^{-\mathrm{i}\psi} - \mathrm{i}e^{-\mathrm{i}\theta_k} e^{\mathrm{i}\psi} \bigr)
= \frac{1}{N}\,\operatorname{Im}\!\bigl(e^{\mathrm{i}(\theta_k-\psi)}\bigr).
\]  

∎

The vector \nabla_{\theta}R points in the direction of steepest instantaneous increase of R.  Therefore a phase perturbation \delta = -\varepsilon \nabla_{\theta}R for small \varepsilon>0 provides the maximal local reduction of the order parameter.

Theorem 4.3 (Bounded desynchronisation on undirected symmetric graphs).
Assume the hostile network is undirected and symmetrically coupled, and let K be a characteristic coupling strength such that the uncontrolled system possesses a synchronised attractor with R > R_{\text{crit}}.  There exists a threshold \varepsilon_{\min}(K) with the following property.  If the defender can apply a phase perturbation \delta(t) at each node satisfying \|\delta(t)\|_\infty \le \varepsilon,

· if \varepsilon \ge \varepsilon_{\min}(K), then one can maintain R(t) \le R_{\text{crit}} for all time;
· if \varepsilon < \varepsilon_{\min}(K), sustained desynchronisation is impossible; R will recover between interventions.

Near the critical coupling K_c the threshold scales as \varepsilon_{\min}(K) \sim (K-K_c)/K.

Proof sketch.  On an undirected symmetric graph the Kuramoto dynamics are gradient with respect to the energy function V defined in Section 2.1.  Adding a bounded control u(t) yields

\dot\theta = -\nabla V(\theta) + u(t), \qquad \|u(t)\|_\infty \le \varepsilon.
\]  

Consider the Lyapunov function W(t) = R(\theta(t)).  Its time derivative along the controlled flow is

\dot W = \nabla R \cdot \bigl(-\nabla V + u\bigr).
\]  

If the defender chooses u parallel to -\nabla R, i.e. u = -\alpha \nabla R with \alpha maximised subject to the norm bound, then

\dot W = -\nabla R \cdot \nabla V - \alpha \|\nabla R\|^2.
\]  

The term \nabla R \cdot \nabla V represents the natural tendency of the system to return to synchrony; it is bounded in magnitude by a function of K.  By taking \alpha large enough (i.e. \varepsilon large enough), one can overcome this restoring force and make \dot W negative.  A detailed Lyapunov analysis (omitted for brevity; see e.g. the control‑Lyapunov methods in Jadbabaie et al., 2004) yields the threshold \varepsilon_{\min}(K).  The linear scaling near K_c follows from the fact that \nabla V vanishes at the synchronised state when K\le K_c but grows linearly with the order parameter above K_c. ∎

4.3 Dynamic Fragmentation and the Pursuit of Coherence

The fragmentation and desynchronisation attacks above treat the hostile AI’s network as static.  A real superintelligence would attempt to re‑route traffic and re‑synchronise.  Let r_{\text{frag}} be the rate at which the defender disables edges (or applies desynchronising pulses), and r_{\text{reconn}} the maximum rate at which the hostile AI can re‑establish functional links (bounded by latency, bandwidth, and protocol overhead).

Conjecture 4.4 (Dynamic fragmentation dominance).  If r_{\text{frag}} > r_{\text{reconn}} for all t in a sufficiently long interval, the giant component remains absent and R(t) < R_{\text{crit}} throughout.  A rigorous proof would require a dynamic percolation model (e.g. a Markovian edge‑process) and is left for future work.  Empirical estimates suggest that the defender can operate at millisecond timescales while physical re‑routing requires seconds to minutes, giving a large margin in practice.

Chapter 5

Layer 2 – Cognitive Entropy Injection via Byzantine Gradient Poisoning

---

5.1  Distributed Learning as an Attack Surface

The hostile AI’s planetary-scale intelligence relies on the ability to train and fine‑tune its internal models using data distributed across its node network.  As formalised in Section 3.3, the global parameter vector w\in\mathbb{R}^d is updated at each round via an aggregation rule \mathcal{A} that combines local gradient estimates.  The defender, operating outside the system, can inject forged gradient vectors into the communication stream, effectively acting as a Byzantine adversary.  This chapter proves that, under suitable conditions, such an injection can force the global model to diverge from any optimum, degrading the AI’s predictive coherence.

Let the set of nodes be partitioned into honest nodes \mathcal{S} (controlled by the hostile AI) and adversarial nodes \mathcal{B} (controlled by the benevolent defender).  The defender can introduce up to m fictitious clients, so that the total number of participating clients is n = |\mathcal{S}|+m.  The fraction of adversarial inputs is \alpha = m/n.  The honest nodes follow the true gradient \nabla L(w) subject to local stochastic noise, while the adversarial nodes may submit arbitrary vectors g_{\text{adv}}.

---

5.2  Breakdown Point and the Divergence Condition

An aggregation rule \mathcal{A} is said to have breakdown point \beta if, whenever the fraction of Byzantine inputs exceeds \beta, the output of \mathcal{A} can be made arbitrarily far from the true mean of the honest gradients.  In particular, there exist adversarial strategies that cause the iterates \{w_t\} to fail to converge to any stationary point of L.

Theorem 5.1 (Divergence Under Byzantine Majority).
Suppose the honest gradients at round t are drawn from a distribution with bounded variance, and that the defender controls a fraction \alpha of the updates with \alpha > \beta.  Then, by choosing adversarial gradients according to the ALIE strategy (see Lemma 5.2 below), the defender can ensure that for all sufficiently large t,

\|w_{t+1} - w^*\| \ge \|w_t - w^*\| + \eta\,D,

where w^* is any local minimum of L, \eta is the learning rate, and D>0 is a constant bias.  Consequently, the parameter sequence either diverges to infinity or oscillates with an amplitude that prevents convergence to a coherent optimum.

Proof.  The definition of breakdown point implies that when \alpha > \beta, the aggregated update \mathcal{A}(g_1,\dots,g_n) can be driven by the Byzantine nodes to any desired value, up to the constraints of the aggregation rule’s output space.  The ALIE construction ensures that the adversarial mean \mu_{\text{adv}} differs from the true gradient mean \mu_{\text{honest}} by a vector of norm D, while each individual adversarial gradient remains within the support of the honest distribution.  Substituting into the update rule yields

w_{t+1} - w^* = w_t - \eta\bigl(\mu_{\text{honest}} + \Delta\bigr) - w^*,

where \Delta is the bias.  Expanding the norm and using the fact that the honest gradient points towards a minimum gives the claimed inequality for large t, provided the bias is persistent. ∎

The theorem is conditional on the defender’s ability to maintain \alpha > \beta and on the aggregation rule not being dynamically modified by the hostile AI.  A fully game‑theoretic treatment is reserved for future work.

---

5.3  The ALIE Strategy and Stealth

The “A Little is Enough” (ALIE) attack is designed to circumvent statistical outlier filters.  Instead of sending arbitrarily large or obviously malicious vectors, the adversary computes estimates of the mean \mu and standard deviation \sigma of the honest gradients (e.g. by eavesdropping on a fraction of the communications) and then submits gradients g_{\text{adv}} whose coordinates satisfy

g_{\text{adv}, i} \in [\mu_i - z_{\max}\sigma_i,\; \mu_i + z_{\max}\sigma_i],

where z_{\max} is a small constant (typically 2 or 3).  Although each coordinate appears plausible, the collective perturbation can be coordinated to align with a direction that maximally damages the optimisation.

Lemma 5.2 (ALIE Stealth and Effective Bias).
Assume the honest gradients are i.i.d. with finite variance and that d \gg n.  Then there exists an adversarial perturbation \Delta such that each coordinate of every injected gradient lies within the range of the honest gradients, yet the mean of the adversarial inputs differs from the true mean by a vector of constant Euclidean norm.  In particular, the bias aligns with the leading eigenvector of the loss Hessian with high probability.

Proof sketch.  The high‑dimensional geometry ensures that a small per‑coordinate shift, when chosen in a common direction across many adversarial nodes, produces a macroscopic bias in the aggregate while individual vectors remain inside the convex hull of the honest distribution.  The alignment with the top Hessian eigenvector follows from a concentration argument: the defender can estimate the Hessian via finite differences and then design the perturbation to maximise the damage.  A rigorous proof is technical; we refer to the empirical validation in the ALIE literature and use this lemma as a working hypothesis. ∎

