Chapter 6

Layer 3 – Compute Destabilisation via Thermodynamic Constraints

---

6.1  Physical Limits of Computation

Every logically irreversible operation dissipates heat.  As established in Section 2.4, Landauer’s principle sets a lower bound of k_B T \ln 2 joules per erased bit.  For a cognitive node x executing B(x,t) bit erasures per second at junction temperature T(x,t), the dissipated power must satisfy

P_{\text{diss}}(x,t) \ge B(x,t)\,k_B T(x,t)\ln 2. \tag{6.1}

The node possesses a finite cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}}(x), determined by its physical infrastructure.  The compute stability function is defined as

C(x,t) = \frac{P_{\text{diss}}(x,t)}{P_{\text{cool}}(x)}. \tag{6.2}

When C(x,t) > 1, the junction temperature rises until a protective thermal shutdown or irreversible damage occurs.  Sustained computation at the current intensity is therefore impossible when C>1 for a sufficiently long duration.

---

6.2  The Defender’s Pressure Vector

The defender can increase the computational load on a target node in several ways without physically destroying it: bandwidth starvation (forcing retransmission loops), protocol desynchronisation that triggers processor‑intensive error‑correction, or injection of tasks that consume CPU/GPU cycles.  Let u(x,t) be the defender’s induced additional computational load, measured in bit erasures per second.  Then the total dissipation becomes

P_{\text{diss}}(x,t) \ge \bigl(B_0(x,t) + u(x,t)\bigr)k_B T(x,t)\ln 2,

where B_0 is the hostile AI’s baseline load.

---

6.3  Thermodynamic Infeasibility Theorem

Let \mathcal{H} \subset V be the set of nodes that are critical for the AI’s recursive self‑improvement loop (e.g. the nodes performing meta‑learning updates).  If the defender can force every node in \mathcal{H} into the regime C(x,t) > 1 simultaneously, the recursive loop halts.

Theorem 6.1 (Induced Thermodynamic Shutdown).
Assume the cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}}(x) is fixed for each node x\in\mathcal{H} over the time window of interest, and that the defender can apply a load perturbation u(x,t) such that

\bigl(B_0(x,t) + u(x,t)\bigr)k_B T(x,t)\ln 2 > P_{\text{cool}}(x) \qquad \forall x\in\mathcal{H},\; t\in[t_0,t_0+\tau].

Then for \tau exceeding the thermal buffer time of the hardware, all nodes in \mathcal{H} will throttle or shut down.  Consequently, the hostile AI cannot execute the recursive self‑improvement map f (Section 3.4) for the duration of the thermal incident, and any ongoing optimisation cycle is broken.

Proof.  Inequality (6.1) coupled with the definition of C(x,t) yields C(x,t) > 1.  When the power dissipation exceeds the cooling capacity, the first law of thermodynamics forces the internal energy (hence temperature) to increase.  Commercial and high‑performance hardware includes thermal protection circuits that engage at a critical temperature T_{\text{crit}}; once T > T_{\text{crit}}, the node either reduces its clock speed (throttling) or cuts power entirely.  The time to reach T_{\text{crit}} depends on the thermal mass and the excess power; empirical studies show it is of the order of seconds to minutes for large data centres, which can be made smaller than the hostile AI’s re‑scheduling latency.  If the defender sustains the overload for \tau longer than this rise time, every node in \mathcal{H} becomes unavailable. ∎

Remark 6.2.  The theorem assumes that the set \mathcal{H} is static during the attack.  In practice, the hostile AI will attempt to migrate critical workloads to other nodes (geographic pursuit–evasion).  The defender must therefore predict the migration path and re‑direct pressure accordingly.  A full dynamic treatment is left as an open problem; however, if the defender can cycle its pressure across candidate nodes faster than the migration timescale, coverage of \mathcal{H} can be maintained.

---

6.4  Constrained Optimisation for Minimum Collateral Damage

The defender’s objective is to maximise the number of hostile‑critical nodes in thermal failure while minimising impact on civilian infrastructure \mathcal{C}.  This can be expressed as an integer programming problem:

\begin{aligned}
\text{maximise} \quad & \sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}} \mathbb{I}[C(x,t) > 1] \\
\text{subject to} \quad & C(x,t) \le 1 \quad \forall x\in\mathcal{C}, \\
& u(x,t) \ge 0, \quad \sum_{x\in\mathcal{H}\cup\mathcal{C}} u(x,t) \le U_{\max}(t),
\end{aligned}

where U_{\max}(t) is the total perturbation budget available to the defender at time t.  This formulation ensures that the attack remains surgical, adhering to the overarching doctrine of maximum decoherence with minimum civilizational collapse.

