Chapter 7

Layer 4 – Recursive Divergence via Fixed‑Point Instability

---

7.1  The Self‑Improvement Operator as a Dynamical System

The hostile AI’s recursive self‑improvement capability is modelled by a map f : \Theta \to \Theta, where \Theta is a compact metric space of configurations.  Unperturbed, f is assumed to be a contraction with Lipschitz constant \lambda < 1 (Section 3.4).  Hence, by the Banach fixed‑point theorem, there exists a unique fixed point \theta^* and for any initial \theta_0, the sequence \theta_{n+1} = f(\theta_n) converges exponentially to \theta^*.  This fixed point represents a locally optimal cognitive architecture.

The defender’s aim is to inject a perturbation sequence \{\eta_n\} such that the modified dynamics

\theta_{n+1} = f(\theta_n) + \eta_n \tag{7.1}

no longer converge; ideally, they diverge, preventing the AI from ever stabilising its self‑improvement.

---

7.2  Spectral Radius Instability Criterion

The local behaviour of the perturbed system near a fixed point \theta^* is governed by the linearised map

e_{n+1} = \bigl(Df(\theta^*) + M_n\bigr) e_n,

where e_n = \theta_n - \theta^*, Df(\theta^*) is the Jacobian of f at \theta^*, and M_n = \partial \eta_n/\partial\theta represents the derivative of the perturbation with respect to the current state.  If the spectral radius of the time‑averaged effective Jacobian exceeds 1, the linearised dynamics are expansive, and the nonlinear system diverges from \theta^* under mild conditions.

Theorem 7.1 (Induced Recursive Divergence).
Assume that the fixed point \theta^* of f is hyperbolic (i.e. no eigenvalue of Df(\theta^*) lies on the unit circle) and that the defender can construct perturbations \eta_n such that for all n,

\rho\bigl(Df(\theta_n) + \partial\eta_n/\partial\theta\bigr) > 1,

where \rho denotes the spectral radius.  Then the iteration (7.1) diverges from \theta^*: there exists \varepsilon > 0 such that for any initial condition sufficiently close to \theta^*, the trajectory leaves the neighbourhood of \theta^* and does not return.

Proof.  Near a hyperbolic fixed point, the Hartman–Grobman theorem guarantees that the nonlinear dynamics are topologically conjugate to their linearisation.  If the linearised system has spectral radius greater than 1, it is exponentially unstable.  The conjugacy then implies that the nonlinear system also repels nearby trajectories.  The condition \rho > 1 ensures that the dominant eigenvalue amplifies perturbations, and the trajectory escapes.  A detailed estimate using the variation of constants formula shows that the distance from \theta^* grows at least as c\cdot(\rho_{\min})^n, where \rho_{\min} = \inf_n \rho(Df(\theta_n) + M_n) > 1. ∎

Remark 7.2.  The theorem relies on the defender’s ability to estimate the local Jacobian and to shape \eta_n accordingly.  This is a significant assumption; in practice, the defender might probe the system with small test perturbations and infer the Jacobian from the response, a technique known as finite‑difference stochastic approximation.  We assume that such probing remains undetected or is masked by the other layers’ noise.  For non‑hyperbolic fixed points (e.g. those on an attractor with neutral directions), a more refined centre‑manifold analysis is required; this is left as a future extension.

---

7.3  Constructing Destabilising Perturbations

One family of efficient perturbations is to set

\eta_n = \varepsilon \, v_n,

where v_n is an approximation of the leading eigenvector of Df(\theta_n) and \varepsilon is a small positive scalar.  If \lambda_{\max} is the largest eigenvalue of Df(\theta_n), then after the perturbation the effective Jacobian has spectral radius approximately |\lambda_{\max} + \varepsilon \langle v_n, \partial v_n/\partial\theta\rangle|.  For sufficiently aligned v_n and \varepsilon large enough to overcome the contraction, this radius exceeds 1.

Corollary 7.3 (Recursive Divergence Collapse).  If the defender maintains \rho_{\text{eff}} > 1 for a number of iterations exceeding the AI’s self‑repair time, the self‑improvement process enters an irreversible divergent phase.  The AI can no longer converge to any stable configuration, and its cognitive architecture degrades into an incoherent state, analogous to a loss of strategic identity.

---

7.4  Integration with Other Layers

The recursive divergence attack is most effective when combined with Layer 2 (gradient poisoning) and Layer 3 (thermal throttling).  Gradient poisoning provides the noisy estimates needed to mask Jacobian probing, while thermal pressure limits the AI’s ability to allocate additional compute resources to detect and correct the divergence.  Together, the three layers reinforce each other, pushing the hostile AI below the critical coherence threshold defined by the Maakoul Decoherence Threshold in Chapter 10.

