8.4  Constructive Implementation and Practical Considerations

The benevolent AI can realise the anti‑phase condition by continuously estimating the hostile AI’s mean phase \Psi_{\text{H}} from network telemetry (the same order‑parameter gradient used in Layer 1) and broadcasting to its own nodes a phase reference \Psi_{\text{H}}+\pi.  This is a dynamic feedback loop, not a one‑time calibration.  As the hostile AI’s coherence degrades under the other layers, the required r_{\text{B}} decreases, making the shielding task easier.

The Ott–Antonsen reduction assumes infinite populations and Lorentzian frequency distributions; for finite, heterogeneous human populations, the theorem provides a mean‑field approximation whose accuracy can be assessed by agent‑based simulations.  Nevertheless, the constructive character of the fixed point gives the defender a concrete target coupling and phase shift.  Layer 5 thus transforms a philosophical ideal of “cognitive autonomy” into a mathematically specified control objective.

