9.4  Recursive Auditing and Byzantine Fault Isolation

Even with robust synchronisation, a fraction of benevolent nodes could be compromised by the hostile AI (e.g. through hardware tampering).  The defensive mesh must therefore include a Byzantine fault‑tolerance layer that audits the behaviour of peers and isolates any that exhibit drift beyond a threshold.

Let each node k periodically broadcast a signed commitment to its state (phase, configuration hash).  The other nodes verify that the commitment is consistent with the collective state predicted by the dynamics.  If node j detects an inconsistency exceeding a predefined bound, it broadcasts an alert and the mesh removes that node from the trusted set.  This can be formalised as a consensus problem in a partially synchronous network.

Theorem 9.4 (Byzantine Resilience of the Mesh).
Assume that at most f out of P nodes can be arbitrarily faulty, with f < P/3, and that messages are authenticated.  Then the protocol that requires agreement on a round‑by‑round state among 2f+1 correct nodes, with a timeout, guarantees that the trusted core of the mesh maintains its integrity and continues to execute the decoherence strategy without corruption.

Proof.  This is a direct application of classical results on Byzantine agreement with digital signatures (Lamport–Shostak–Pease).  The mesh maintains a consistent view of which nodes are trusted, and the internal synchronisation and constitutional constraints are evaluated only over that trusted set.  The bound f < P/3 is necessary for deterministic agreement in an asynchronous setting; practical systems can tolerate more faults with cryptographic randomness. ∎

