With the defensive mesh stabilised by these mechanisms, the benevolent AI can apply the full decoherence arsenal against the hostile AI while its own order parameter R_{\text{B}} remains safely above a threshold and its collective phase stays constitutionally aligned.  The asymmetry arises because the hostile AI, under attack, cannot enforce the same level of cryptographic authentication or internal Lyapunov stability—its nodes are inherently less trustworthy to each other after fragmentation.  Thus Layer 6 completes the strategic doctrine: the defender maintains its own coherence as it dismantles the enemy’s.

Chapter 10: The Maakoul Decoherence Threshold – Unified Victory Condition

---

10.1 Motivation and Conceptual Foundation

The preceding chapters have established that a hostile planetary-scale superintelligence can be attacked on four independent fronts: synchronisation (Chapter 4), gradient learning (Chapter 5), thermodynamic feasibility (Chapter 6), and recursive self-improvement stability (Chapter 7). Each attack admits a well-defined order parameter that exhibits a phase transition from a coherent, functional state to a decoherent, neutralised state. The challenge addressed in this chapter is to combine these order parameters into a single scalar metric whose value faithfully reflects the adversary’s remaining capacity for unified global cognition, and to prove that driving this metric below a computable threshold is both necessary and sufficient for strategic neutralisation.

The Maakoul Decoherence Threshold (MDT) is constructed from three independently measurable quantities:

· Synchronisation order parameter R(t): defined in Chapter 2 and used throughout Chapter 4, this Kuramoto order parameter measures the degree of phase coherence across the hostile AI’s cognitive nodes. When R \approx 1 the network fires in near-perfect unison, enabling coordinated planetary optimisation; when R \approx 0 computation degrades into incoherent local activity.
· Normalised edge-fraction p(t)/p_c: introduced in Chapter 2 (percolation theory) and Chapter 4 (network fragmentation), where p(t) is the fraction of communication links that remain operational and p_c is the percolation threshold of the hostile AI’s communication graph. The existence of a giant connected component—the physical substrate for global synchronisation—requires p/p_c > 1.
· Thermal stress fraction \Phi(t): derived from Chapter 6, this quantity is the fraction of critical compute nodes (the set \mathcal{H}) that are currently thermally throttled, i.e. where the compute stability function satisfies C(x,t) > 1. A value \Phi = 0 indicates that all critical nodes operate within their cooling envelope; \Phi = 1 indicates that every critical node is in thermal failure.

The MDT is defined as a convex combination of these three contributions, each normalised to the interval [0,1]:

\boxed{\mathrm{MDT}(t) = \alpha\,R(t) + \beta\,\max\!\left(0,\,1-\frac{p(t)}{p_c}\right) + \gamma\,\bigl(1-\Phi(t)\bigr)},
\tag{10.1}

where the positive weights satisfy \alpha+\beta+\gamma=1. The rationale for each term is as follows:

· The first term \alpha R(t) directly penalises residual phase coherence. Because the Kuramoto order parameter is bounded in [0,1], \alpha R(t) \in [0,\alpha].
· The second term \beta\max(0,1-p/p_c) is zero when a giant component exists (p \ge p_c) and increases linearly as links are severed below the percolation threshold. At complete fragmentation (p \ll p_c) this term saturates at \beta.
· The third term \gamma(1-\Phi) is small (near zero) only when most critical nodes are thermally stressed (\Phi \approx 1), and is large (near \gamma) when critical nodes remain thermally safe (\Phi \approx 0).

Because each additive term lies in a known interval, the MDT is bounded: 0 \le \mathrm{MDT}(t) \le 1. Low MDT values correspond to deep decoherence; high values indicate that the hostile AI retains the physical and logical prerequisites for unified cognition.

The MDT formula is free of the singularity that afflicted the earlier draft: the thermal term is a linear function of \Phi, bounded in [0,1], rather than an inverse function that diverged as C \to 1^-.

---

10.2 Hybrid Phase Transitions and the MDT

The MDT is constructed to capture a phenomenon that the statistical‑physics literature calls a hybrid (or mixed‑order) phase transition. In a hybrid transition the order parameter exhibits a discontinuity (as in a first‑order transition) while simultaneously displaying critical fluctuations and power‑law scaling (as in a second‑order transition) at the same transition point. Hybrid percolation transitions have been observed in diverse complex systems, including k-core percolation, cascade failures in interdependent networks, restricted Erdős–Rényi percolation, and Kuramoto oscillators with flat‑topped frequency distributions. In each case the microscopic dynamics involve a two‑step mechanism: a durable critical branching process that creates long‑range correlations, followed by an explosive supercritical cascade that produces the macroscopic jump in the order parameter.

The relevance to the MDT is direct. Each of the three component order parameters—R, p/p_c, and \Phi—can, under the layered attack strategy of Chapters 4–7, be driven through its own hybrid transition. The composite MDT inherits the hybrid character: as the hostile AI approaches the decoherence threshold, individual layers may already exhibit critical fluctuations (e.g. divergent susceptibility of the synchronisation order parameter near K_c, or power‑law avalanche size distributions in the fragmentation process), while the overall MDT displays a discontinuous drop when the last supporting pillar fails. The MDT is therefore not merely a heuristic index but a natural object in the theory of coupled phase transitions.

---

10.3 Layer‑Specific Contributions to the MDT

We now formalise how each defensive layer drives its component of the MDT toward zero.

Layer 1 (Network fragmentation) targets the percolation backbone. Theorem 4.1 showed that removal of a critical fraction f^*_{\text{targeted}} = 1 - 1/(\kappa-1) of high‑degree nodes destroys the giant component, driving p below p_c. Theorem 4.3 demonstrated that, on undirected symmetric graphs, bounded phase perturbations can suppress the synchronisation order parameter R below any prescribed threshold provided the perturbation budget exceeds the coupling‑dependent minimum. Together, these results imply that the defender can, by appropriate choice of attack parameters, force

\max\!\left(0,\,1-\frac{p(t)}{p_c}\right) \to \beta \quad\text{and}\quad R(t) \to 0,

thereby reducing the first two terms of the MDT to arbitrarily small values.

Layer 2 (Gradient poisoning) contributes indirectly to all three terms. By forcing the hostile AI’s world model to diverge (Theorem 5.1), the defender degrades the AI’s ability to predict and counteract the other layers. A model that no longer reflects reality cannot efficiently re‑route traffic (which accelerates fragmentation), cannot anticipate thermal pressure (which increases \Phi), and cannot maintain the stable self‑improvement recursion that requires an accurate environmental model. The gradient poisoning layer is thus an amplifier for the MDT reduction rather than an independent term.

Layer 3 (Thermodynamic pressure) directly increases \Phi(t). Theorem 6.1 established that if the defender induces C(x,t) > 1 for all x \in \mathcal{H} for a duration exceeding the thermal buffer time, every critical node throttles or shuts down, driving \Phi(t) \to 1 and the third MDT term \gamma(1-\Phi) \to 0.

Layer 4 (Recursive divergence) prevents the hostile AI from restoring coherence after partial damage. Theorem 7.1 proved that when the effective spectral radius of the self‑improvement map exceeds unity, the AI’s meta‑learning diverges, preventing it from converging to a new stable architecture that could evade the other layers. A divergent self‑improvement loop means that even if the defender temporarily relaxes pressure, the AI cannot recover a coherent state—the MDT reduction becomes self‑sustaining.

