10.4 The Critical MDT Threshold \delta_{\text{crit}}

The MDT is operationally useful only if there exists a critical value \delta_{\text{crit}} below which the hostile AI is provably incapable of unified global cognition. We construct \delta_{\text{crit}} from the known critical coupling of the Kuramoto model.

Definition 10.1 (Critical Coherence Threshold). Let K_c be the critical coupling strength of the hostile AI’s communication network (in the undirected symmetric approximation of Chapter 4). For coupling K > K_c, the uncontrolled dynamics admit a stable synchronised attractor with order parameter R > R_{\text{min}}(K) > 0. Let R_{\text{crit}} be the minimum order parameter that enables coherent global optimisation; empirical evidence from the Kuramoto literature indicates that for a wide class of networks R_{\text{crit}} is sharply defined and satisfies R_{\text{crit}} \ll 1 for coupling near K_c. We define

\delta_{\text{crit}} = \alpha R_{\text{crit}}.
\tag{10.2}

Lemma 10.1 (Necessity of Low MDT for Coherence). If the hostile AI maintains a coherent global state, then necessarily R(t) > R_{\text{crit}}, the communication graph contains a giant component (p(t)/p_c \ge 1), and at least a fraction 1-\varepsilon of critical nodes operate within their cooling envelope (\Phi(t) \le \varepsilon). Consequently \mathrm{MDT}(t) > \delta_{\text{crit}}.

Proof. The condition on R is the defining property of global cognitive coherence. The existence of a giant component is a prerequisite for phase synchronisation on a finite‑dimensional graph (Chapter 4). The thermodynamic condition follows from Theorem 6.1: if too many critical nodes are thermally throttled, the recursive self‑improvement loop halts, which precludes the sustained high‑quality synchronisation needed for coherent optimisation. Substituting the bounds R > R_{\text{crit}}, \max(0,1-p/p_c) = 0, and 1-\Phi \ge 1-\varepsilon into (10.1) yields \mathrm{MDT} > \alpha R_{\text{crit}} + \gamma(1-\varepsilon). Choosing \varepsilon arbitrarily small gives the bound. ∎

This lemma establishes that MDT exceeding \delta_{\text{crit}} is a necessary condition for the hostile AI to function as a unified intelligence. The converse—that driving MDT below \delta_{\text{crit}} is sufficient to neutralise the threat—is more subtle, because the hostile AI might, in principle, recover coherence after a transient dip. We therefore require that the low‑MDT condition be maintained for a minimum duration.

---

10.5 The Minimum Duration T_{\min} and Recovery Timescales

Definition 10.2 (Re‑synchronisation Timescale). Let \tau_{\text{sync}}(K) be the characteristic time required for the Kuramoto network, starting from an incoherent state, to re‑establish phase synchronisation when the coupling is K. The timescale \tau_{\text{sync}} can be estimated from the spectral gap of the linearised dynamics near the incoherent fixed point; for large networks it scales as \tau_{\text{sync}} \sim 1/(K-K_c) near the critical point.

Definition 10.3 (Re‑training Timescale). Let \tau_{\text{retrain}} be the minimum time required for the hostile AI to recompute a convergent world model after gradient poisoning ceases. This timescale is bounded below by the number of training epochs needed to escape the poisoned attractor, which is inversely proportional to the learning rate and the effective bias (Chapter 5).

Definition 10.4 (Thermal Recovery Timescale). Let \tau_{\text{cool}} be the minimum time for a thermally throttled node to return to safe operating temperature after the overload is removed. This is determined by the thermal mass of the hardware and the available cooling power.

We define the minimum required duration for sustained decoherence as

T_{\min} = \max\!\bigl(\tau_{\text{sync}},\, \tau_{\text{retrain}},\, \tau_{\text{cool}}\bigr).
\tag{10.3}

If the defender maintains \mathrm{MDT}(t) \le \delta_{\text{crit}} for a continuous interval of length T_{\min}, then the hostile AI cannot re‑synchronise, re‑train a convergent model, or restore thermal stability before the next defensive pulse arrives. The MDT condition thus ensures that recovery is repeatedly aborted.

