10.7 MDT Monitoring and Feedback Control

For operational use, the benevolent AI must estimate \mathrm{MDT}(t) in real time from network telemetry. The three inputs are measurable:

· R(t): computed by sampling phase data from a representative subset of hostile nodes (e.g. packet‑timing correlations);
· p(t)/p_c: estimated by passive network tomography to determine the fraction of operational links, combined with prior knowledge of the graph’s degree distribution to infer p_c;
· \Phi(t): measured by monitoring thermal emissions (infrared satellite imagery, power‑grid load data) and inferring which nodes in the predicted set \mathcal{H} are throttled.

The benevolent AI can implement a proportional‑derivative controller that adjusts the attack intensity on each layer to keep \mathrm{MDT}(t) below \delta_{\text{crit}} with minimum energy expenditure. Because the MDT dynamics are slower than the individual layer dynamics (the hybrid phase transition of each component involves critical slowing down near the transition point), the control loop can operate at a feasible timescale.

