B.1  Molloy–Reed Criterion Derivation

We provide a self‑contained derivation of the Molloy–Reed condition for the existence of a giant component in a configuration model graph.  Let the degree distribution be p_k with generating function G_0(x) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty p_k x^k.  The mean degree is \langle k\rangle = G_0'(1).  Following a random edge, the excess degree distribution (the number of additional edges of the node reached) is q_k = (k+1)p_{k+1}/\langle k\rangle, with generating function G_1(x) = G_0'(x)/\langle k\rangle.

The size of the giant component is S = 1 - G_0(u) where u is the smallest non‑negative solution of the fixed‑point equation u = G_1(u).  A non‑trivial solution u < 1 exists if and only if G_1'(1) > 1.  Computing:

G_1'(1) = \frac{1}{\langle k\rangle} G_0''(1) = \frac{\langle k^2\rangle - \langle k\rangle}{\langle k\rangle} = \frac{\langle k^2\rangle}{\langle k\rangle} - 1.

Thus G_1'(1) > 1 is equivalent to \kappa = \langle k^2\rangle/\langle k\rangle > 2, which is the Molloy–Reed criterion.

For targeted removal of the highest‑degree nodes, we truncate the degree distribution at some k_{\max}.  The removed fraction f satisfies f = \sum_{k > k_{\max}} p_k.  The new generating functions are renormalised versions of the original.  The effective \kappa' becomes a decreasing function of f; the critical f^*_{\text{targeted}} is found by setting \kappa' = 2.  Under the assumption that the original distribution has a power‑law tail, this yields the explicit formula f^*_{\text{targeted}} = 1 - 1/(\kappa-1) as in Theorem 4.1.  A rigorous proof can be found in Cohen et al. (2000, 2001).

---

B.2  Control‑Lyapunov Derivation of Desynchronisation Threshold (Theorem 4.3)

We present a more detailed Lyapunov argument.  Consider the undirected Kuramoto model with control:

\dot\theta_j = \omega_j + \sum_{k} K_{jk}\sin(\theta_k-\theta_j) + u_j(t),

where u(t) is chosen by the defender with \|u(t)\|_\infty \le \varepsilon.  The energy function is

V(\theta) = -\frac12\sum_{j,k} K_{jk}\cos(\theta_j-\theta_k).

Its time derivative along the controlled flow is

\dot V = \sum_{j,k} K_{jk}\sin(\theta_j-\theta_k)(\dot\theta_j - \dot\theta_k)
= -\sum_{j,k} K_{jk}\sin(\theta_j-\theta_k)\bigl[(\omega_k-\omega_j) + (u_k-u_j)\bigr] - \sum_{j,k} K_{jk} \sin^2(\theta_j-\theta_k).

The last term is non‑positive and proportional to the phase dispersion.  The defender selects u to make the first sum as negative as possible.  Let \delta = \|u\|_\infty.  The term involving u can be bounded by 2\delta \sum_{j,k} K_{jk} |\sin(\theta_j-\theta_k)|.  Near the synchronised state, |\sin(\theta_j-\theta_k)| \approx |\theta_j-\theta_k|, and the sum scales as the order parameter variance.

Define the Lyapunov function W(R) = \frac12(1-R)^2.  Its derivative is \dot W = -(1-R)\dot R.  From the dynamics, \dot R = \nabla R \cdot (-\nabla V + u).  Near synchrony, \nabla R \cdot \nabla V is positive (restoring), and the defender chooses u = -\varepsilon \nabla R / \|\nabla R\| to oppose it.  The condition for \dot W \le 0 is that \varepsilon \|\nabla R\| \ge \nabla R \cdot \nabla V.  The magnitudes of \nabla R and \nabla V near the critical coupling K_c scale as (K-K_c) and K respectively, leading to the threshold \varepsilon_{\min} \sim (K-K_c)/K.  A complete proof requires a LaSalle invariance argument to ensure convergence to the set where R is small; this is standard in control‑Lyapunov theory.

---

B.3  Ott–Antonsen Reduction for Three Species

We sketch the derivation of the reduced system (8.2) from the full three‑species Kuramoto model.  Consider species S with phases \theta_j^{\text{S}} obeying

\dot\theta_j^{\text{S}} = \omega_j^{\text{S}} + \frac{K_{\text{S}}}{N_{\text{S}}}\sum_{m=1}^{N_{\text{S}}}\sin(\theta_m^{\text{S}}-\theta_j^{\text{S}}) + \sum_{\text{S}'\neq \text{S}} \frac{K_{\text{S},\text{S}'}}{N_{\text{S}'}}\sum_{l=1}^{N_{\text{S}'}}\sin(\theta_l^{\text{S}'}-\theta_j^{\text{S}}).

In the limit N_{\text{S}}\to\infty, we introduce the probability density f^{\text{S}}(\theta,\omega,t) of oscillators with natural frequency \omega.  The continuity equation is

\frac{\partial f^{\text{S}}}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\bigl(v^{\text{S}} f^{\text{S}}\bigr) = 0,

where the velocity v^{\text{S}} is the right‑hand side expressed via the order parameters z^{\text{S}'}(t) = \frac{1}{N_{\text{S}'}}\sum_l e^{\mathrm{i}\theta_l^{\text{S}'}}.

v^{\text{S}} = \omega + \frac{K_{\text{S}}}{2\mathrm{i}}\bigl(z^{\text{S}} e^{-\mathrm{i}\theta} - \bar z^{\text{S}} e^{\mathrm{i}\theta}\bigr) + \sum_{\text{S}'\neq \text{S}} \frac{K_{\text{S},\text{S}'}}{2\mathrm{i}}\bigl(z^{\text{S}'} e^{-\mathrm{i}\theta} - \bar z^{\text{S}'} e^{\mathrm{i}\theta}\bigr).

Assume f^{\text{S}} has the Fourier expansion f^{\text{S}} = \frac{g^{\text{S}}(\omega)}{2\pi}\bigl(1 + \sum_{n\ge 1} [\alpha^{\text{S}}(\omega,t)^n e^{\mathrm{i}n\theta} + \text{c.c.}]\bigr) with |\alpha^{\text{S}}|<1.  Substituting into the continuity equation yields the evolution equation for \alpha^{\text{S}}:

\frac{\partial \alpha^{\text{S}}}{\partial t} = -\mathrm{i}\omega\alpha^{\text{S}} + \frac{K_{\text{S}}}{2}\bigl(z^{\text{S}} - (\bar z^{\text{S}})(\alpha^{\text{S}})^2\bigr) + \sum_{\text{S}'\neq \text{S}} \frac{K_{\text{S},\text{S}'}}{2}\bigl(z^{\text{S}'} - (\bar z^{\text{S}'})(\alpha^{\text{S}})^2\bigr).

The order parameter is z^{\text{S}}(t) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\alpha^{\text{S}}(\omega,t)}\, g^{\text{S}}(\omega)\,\mathrm{d}\omega.  With a Lorentzian distribution g^{\text{S}}(\omega) = \frac{\Delta_{\text{S}}}{\pi[(\omega-\Omega_{\text{S}})^2 + \Delta_{\text{S}}^2]}, the integral can be evaluated by contour integration, closing in the lower half‑plane, where \alpha^{\text{S}}(\omega,t) is analytic.  The pole at \omega = \Omega_{\text{S}} - \mathrm{i}\Delta_{\text{S}} yields z^{\text{S}}(t) = \overline{\alpha^{\text{S}}(\Omega_{\text{S}}-\mathrm{i}\Delta_{\text{S}},t)}.  Substituting this into the \alpha^{\text{S}} equation and evaluating at the pole gives the closed system (8.2).  This technique is standard; see Ott & Antonsen (2008, 2009).

