B.4  Byzantine Resilience and the ALIE Attack: A Formal Bound

We provide a precise statement of the ALIE effectiveness condition.  In a distributed SGD setting with n clients, let the honest gradients g_i be i.i.d. with mean \mu and covariance \Sigma.  The adversary knows \mu,\Sigma and injects m Byzantine gradients.  The aggregation rule is coordinate‑wise median.  The adversary chooses each coordinate of its gradients as \mu_i + z_{\max} \sigma_i \cdot \operatorname{sgn}(v_i), where v is a chosen direction, and z_{\max} is a constant (e.g., 2.5).  Then, under the condition m > n/2, the median of each coordinate will be shifted from \mu_i by an amount that grows with z_{\max} and the alignment of v with the principal components of \Sigma.  The effective bias D in Theorem 5.1 can be bounded below by c\, z_{\max} \sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(\Sigma)/d} for some constant c>0.  This result, while not a closed‑form theorem for all aggregators, is supported by extensive numerical evidence (Baruch et al., 2019) and is used as a working hypothesis in our framework.

