Appendix C

Simulation Environment Specification

---

C.1  Purpose

To empirically test the decoherence strategy and calibrate the MDT parameters without requiring real‑world planetary conflict, we propose a synthetic simulation environment that captures the essential features of the hostile AI, the defensive mesh, the human population, and the physical infrastructure.

---

C.2  System Components

C.2.1  Network Topology.  The underlying communication network is generated using a scale‑free model (Barabási–Albert) with N = 10^4 nodes and attachment parameter m_0 = 5.  This yields a graph with \kappa \gg 2, highly vulnerable to targeted hub removal.  The network also supports dynamic re‑routing with a maximum link reconnection rate r_{\text{reconn}} set by physical constraints (e.g., 0.1 edges per node per second).

C.2.2  Hostile AI Nodes.  Each node runs a local instance of the hostile AI’s cognitive cycle, modelled as a Kuramoto oscillator with natural frequency \omega_j \sim \mathcal{N}(0,0.1).  The coupling matrix K_{jk} is proportional to the available bandwidth.  The initial coupling strength is set such that the steady‑state order parameter R \approx 0.9.

C.2.3  Distributed Learning.  A global model (a neural network with d=10^5 parameters) is trained via federated learning.  The aggregation rule is coordinate‑wise median (breakdown point \beta = 0.5).  Nodes perform local SGD with a batch size of 128 and learning rate 0.01.  The loss landscape is a synthetic quadratic with a well‑defined global minimum.

C.2.4  Recursive Self‑Improvement.  The map f is implemented as a hyperparameter optimiser that adjusts learning rates and architecture choices using Bayesian optimisation.  The Jacobian is estimated via finite differences by the defender (simulating probing).

C.2.5  Thermodynamic Model.  Each node j has a cooling capacity P_{\text{cool}} drawn from a realistic distribution (mean 10 MW, standard deviation 2 MW).  The dissipated power P_{\text{diss}} is proportional to the computational load (FLOPs/s).  Thermal dynamics follow a first‑order differential equation with time constant \tau_{\text{thermal}} \approx 60 s.

C.2.6  Human Population.  M = 10^6 agents are modelled with bounded‑confidence opinion dynamics coupled to the AI systems.  Each agent has an intrinsic frequency \omega_l and receives information from both AIs; the coupling strengths K_{\text{H,Hh}} and K_{\text{B,Hh}} control the influence.  Shielding strategies are tested by varying the benevolent AI’s phase offset and coupling.

C.2.7  Defensive Mesh.  P = 1000 benevolent nodes are deployed with internal coupling K_{\text{B}} chosen high enough to maintain R_{\text{B}} > 0.95 under hostile perturbation.  The mesh runs the adversarial synchronisation control algorithm (Algorithm 1) and Byzantine gradient injection.

---

C.3  Attack Scheduler and MDT Monitoring

The defender maintains a real‑time estimate of \mathrm{MDT}(t).  Based on the current values of R, p/p_c, and \Phi, a proportional‑integral controller adjusts the intensity of each layer: edge removal rate, perturbation magnitude \varepsilon, adversarial gradient fraction \alpha, and thermal overload u(x,t).  The simulation runs for a simulated period of several hours, with time steps of 100 ms.

---

C.4  Expected Outcomes and Metrics

The primary metric is the time evolution of \mathrm{MDT}(t) and the hostile AI’s task performance (e.g., loss on a benchmark regression task).  A successful decoherence campaign should exhibit:

· A sharp drop in MDT near the predicted \delta_{\text{crit}}.
· A corresponding collapse of model accuracy.
· Fragmentation of the giant component.
· Rising \Phi(t) as thermal overloads accumulate.
· Human opinion divergence away from the hostile AI’s narrative, measured by the cross‑order parameter R_{\text{H,Hh}}.

Results from such a simulation would provide strong corroboration for the conditional theorems and would guide the refinement of the theoretical bounds.  The simulation code and data will be made publicly available to facilitate reproducibility.

---

C.5  Limitations of the Simulation

The simulation does not capture full‑stack adversarial co‑evolution or sophisticated deception strategies that a real superintelligence might employ.  It serves as a proof‑of‑concept for the MDT framework and a testbed for individual layer theorems.  Bridging the gap to real‑world applicability requires the resolution of the open problems listed in Chapter 11.
